KG42.register("rules-e8e8c020f96a05a65e949f4c",{"id":"46-commercial_building_transactive_control::v141-c-R004","paper":"46-commercial_building_transactive_control","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"The clearing function returns the price at its computed intersection as the clearing price. Scope: Central-HVAC transactive market-clearing function. Condition: An intersection between the demand curves and the supply curve exists. The clearing function returns the lowest price value in the demand curve as its clearing-price output. Scope: Central-HVAC clearing function's no-intersection fallback. Condition: No intersection exists and the demand curve is always below the supply curve. The clearing function returns the highest price value in the demand curve as its clearing-price output. Scope: Central-HVAC clearing function's no-intersection fallback. Condition: No intersection exists and the demand curve is always above the supply curve. In both reported cases, the market clearing price is \\hat{\\lambda}. Scope: Reported central-HVAC evaluation cases with baseline-equivalent operation. Condition: In the reported operation, either the demand–supply intersection price is below \\hat{\\lambda}, or there is no intersection. TC sets the market clearing price to the lowest value in this case. Scope: Reported central-HVAC load-following evaluation. Condition: Actual power is below desired power in a reported case where the studied building lacks sufficient power flexibility for demand following.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E001","source":"clearing.local","target":"pricing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001","v141-c-R004-C002","v141-c-R004-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E025","v141-b-g02-E026"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Central-HVAC clearing function's no-intersection fallback. | Central-HVAC transactive market-clearing function. | Reported central-HVAC evaluation cases with baseline-equivalent operation. | Reported central-HVAC load-following evaluation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["ev-v141-b-g03-013","v141-b-g02-E025","v141-b-g02-E026","v141-b-g04-E01","v141-b-g04-E02","v141-b-g04-E03"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The clearing function returns the price at its computed intersection as the clearing price. Scope: Central-HVAC transactive market-clearing function. Condition: An intersection between the demand curves and the supply curve exists.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E025"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The clearing function returns the lowest price value in the demand curve as its clearing-price output. Scope: Central-HVAC clearing function's no-intersection fallback. Condition: No intersection exists and the demand curve is always below the supply curve.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E026"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The clearing function returns the highest price value in the demand curve as its clearing-price output. Scope: Central-HVAC clearing function's no-intersection fallback. Condition: No intersection exists and the demand curve is always above the supply curve.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E026"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"In both reported cases, the market clearing price is \\hat{\\lambda}. Scope: Reported central-HVAC evaluation cases with baseline-equivalent operation. Condition: In the reported operation, either the demand–supply intersection price is below \\hat{\\lambda}, or there is no intersection.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["ev-v141-b-g03-013"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"TC sets the market clearing price to the lowest value in this case. Scope: Reported central-HVAC load-following evaluation. Condition: Actual power is below desired power in a reported case where the studied building lacks sufficient power flexibility for demand following.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E01","v141-b-g04-E02","v141-b-g04-E03"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"ev-v141-b-g03-013","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p11-c2r2__v141-a-M01-activation","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"4.3.2. Demand limiting Fig. 16 illustrates performance of the TC approach when providing the demand ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 17","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In addition, for a significant period of time (e.g., from 6:00 to 10:00 a.m.), the power of the central system under the TC approach is the same as the baseline power. This is because either (1) the price of the intersection between the supply curve and the demand curve is less than \\hat{\\lambda} or (2) there is no intersection. In both cases, \\hat{\\lambda} is the market clearing price resulting in the same cooling temperature set points as those of the baseline operation, as illustrated in Fig. 17."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E025","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1. Python classes/functions for implementing transactive control","anchor_text":"operation status and set point. Method 2 is used for converting the of aggregated demand curves of a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 5","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Function Clearing finds the intersection of two demand curves and the supply curve and returns the price value for the interaction (i.e., clearing price)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E026","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1. Python classes/functions for implementing transactive control","anchor_text":"operation status and set point. Method 2 is used for converting the of aggregated demand curves of a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 5","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"If there is no intersection, Function Clearing returns the lowest price value in the demand curve when the demand curve is always below the supply curve; conversely, it returns the highest price value in the demand curve when the demand curve is always above the supply curve."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p12-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":12,"printed_page":"12","section":"4.3.3. Load following","anchor_text":"challenging without sub-meters. po po 4.3.3. Load following 5 Fig. 19 illustrates the performance of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"This occurs when the studied building does not have sufficient power flexibility for demand following."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p12-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":12,"printed_page":"12","section":"4.3.3. Load following","anchor_text":"challenging without sub-meters. po po 4.3.3. Load following 5 Fig. 19 illustrates the performance of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In such a case, the proposed TC approach sets the market clearing price to the lowest value so that the cooling temperature set points of all zones are set to be the minimum to consume as much power as possible."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-0abd014fd6-p12-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":12,"printed_page":"12","section":"4.3.3. Load following","anchor_text":"challenging without sub-meters. po po 4.3.3. Load following 5 Fig. 19 illustrates the performance of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 19","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Fig. 19 illustrates the performance of the proposed TC approach when providing load following DR service. Specifically, two demand profiles are considered. The first one has a relatively low target (12.68 kW) from 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and a high target (16.48 kW) in the afternoon. The second one has a relatively high target (13.95 kW) in the early morning from 9:00 to 10:00 a.m. and a low target (12.68 kW) for the remainder of the occupied period. The proposed TC approach is capable of following both power profiles for the majority of the control period. However, there are instances when the actual power under the proposed TC approach is lower than the desired power and fails to follow the desired power profile."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":"The rule specifies return behavior of Function Clearing and reported branch outcomes; the price values are computed clearing outputs or reported outcomes, not design-selection or operational decision variables. C1 declared no x objects for this rule, and the source does not define a decision variable over which an agent optimizes here.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed input or constant parameter is declared within this rule's frozen scope. \\hat{\\lambda} appears in C004 as a reported-case clearing-price outcome/condition and is defined elsewhere as the previous-period mean electricity price; treating it as a general parameter of the clearing function would overreach absent C1 scope and B confirmation."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-activation","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation","v141-a-M02-clearing","v141-a-M02-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E025","quote":"Function Clearing finds the intersection of two demand curves and the supply curve and returns the price value for the interaction (i.e., clearing price)."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E026","quote":"If there is no intersection, Function Clearing returns the lowest price value in the demand curve when the demand curve is always below the supply curve"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E026","quote":"If there is no intersection, Function Clearing returns the lowest price value in the demand curve when the demand curve is always below the supply curve; conversely, it returns the highest price value in the demand curve when the demand curve is always above the supply curve."}],[{"evidence_id":"ev-v141-b-g03-013","quote":"This is because either (1) the price of the intersection between the supply curve and the demand curve is less than \\hat{\\lambda} or (2) there is no intersection. In both cases, \\hat{\\lambda} is the market clearing price resulting in the same cooling temperature set points as those of the baseline operation"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E03","quote":"However, there are instances when the actual power under the proposed TC approach is lower than the desired power and fails to follow the desired power profile."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E01","quote":"This occurs when the studied building does not have sufficient power flexibility for demand following."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E02","quote":"In such a case, the proposed TC approach sets the market clearing price to the lowest value so that the cooling temperature set points of all zones are set to be the minimum to consume as much power as possible."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"28dd788016c32fa49271a1eb485e5f696dff483e73f1801446bd86530138b35b","applicability":"needs_revision","reason":"The frozen R004 graph represents only clearing.local and pricing.local with one linking edge, but the source clearing rule is defined by intersection and ordering of demand/supply curves and by returning the lowest or highest price value in the demand curve. Those curve-input and price-value-domain relationships are not represented in R004's V/E, so formalizing C001-C003 or C005 would require inventing curve objects or domains. C004's \\hat{\\lambda} equality is also scoped to reported evaluation cases and the symbol has an unresolved dual use as previous-period mean electricity price versus reported clearing price. B evidence remains unreviewed; this does not assert verified source correctness. Existing phi are retained as procedural not_formalized entries.","bindings":[],"revision_requests":["C.1: Add to v141-c-R004 an explicit graph representation of the demand-curve input(s) and supply-curve input to clearing.local, or explicitly authorize using the existing bidding.demand and bidding.supply nodes within this rule, because C001-C003 depend on curve intersection/order and the current R004 V/E contains only clearing.local and pricing.local.","C.1: Add a graph or typed representation for the price-value set of the demand curve and the clearing-price output returned to pricing.local, including domain/order information needed for 'lowest' and 'highest'; B states those phrases but does not state a numeric price domain.","C.1/B: Re-extract or clarify whether 'two demand curves' in v141-b-g02-E025 means an aggregated demand curve plus supply curve or two separate demand curves; C001 should not silently import an aggregation rule from another rule.","C.1/B: Resolve the identity and scope of \\hat{\\lambda}: Eq. (1) defines it as previous-period mean electricity price, while ev-v141-b-g03-013 calls it the market clearing price in the reported case. Units and the exact reported-case scope are not supplied.","C.1/B: For C005, specify the domain or bound of 'lowest value' in the load-following fallback; B states only 'lowest value' and 'minimum' set point without numeric bounds or an explicit price-domain definition."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
